  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nSurface ridge axis centered across the area provides quiet, \nclear conditions across central and southeast Illinois. Dry air\nand subsidence will continue to produce mainly clear skies and\nlarge diurnal temperature swings over the next several days.\n\nMid-level heights build across the Mississippi Valley Friday \nand this weekend as the surface high slips farther east. Models\nshow strong consensus on establishing persistent southerly \nreturn flow. 850-hPa temperatures in the lower teens C, along \nwith deeper mixing support afternoon highs around 80F on \nSunday, around 15 degrees above climatological averages. \n\nMedium-range deterministic solutions (ECMWF, ECMWF-AI, and GFS)\nand the ensemble suite diverge early next week regarding the \nprogression of an upstream longwave trough and associated \nsurface cold front. The deterministic GFS exhibits a faster \nfrontal transit across central Illinois Monday evening with \nstronger pre-frontal 850-hPa flow (40\u201350 kt). Conversely, the \nECMWF and ECMWF-AI are roughly 6 to 12 hours slower, maintaining\na broader frontal structure and tracking the primary boundary \nthrough the forecast area Tuesday. GEFS and ECMWF ensemble \nmembers favor this slower progression. Latest NBM run has\nlowered PoPs below mentionable levels, but this will likely\nchange as the system develops. Behind the front, a shot of\ncooler air will move in, returning temperatures to more\nseasonable levels Tue-Wed.",
